 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Parallel load",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "While /PL is LOW the levels on D0 to D7 are copied into the eight stages directly, whatever CP and /CE are doing. Q7 then shows D7 and /Q7 its inverse, and a change on D7 reaches the outputs after the D7 to Q7 delay. After /PL goes HIGH again the recovery time trec must pass before the next active clock edge."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Serial shift",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "With /PL HIGH, the level on DS goes into stage 0 and every stage moves up by one on each active edge. The edge is a rising CP while /CE is LOW, or a rising /CE while CP is LOW: CP and /CE are interchangeable clock inputs. The bit that was in stage 6 then appears on Q7."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Clock enable and hold",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "/CE HIGH blocks the clock, as does CP held HIGH; the register then keeps its contents. /CE should go from HIGH to LOW only while CP is LOW."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Outputs",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Only the last stage is brought out, as Q7 and as its complement /Q7. Stages 0 to 6 have no pins."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input levels and overvoltage",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The 74HC165 inputs switch at CMOS levels and the 74HCT165 inputs at TTL levels. The inputs withstand up to 15 V, which lets the part translate a higher-voltage logic signal down to its own supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "TTL-level supply current",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "On the 74HCT165 each input held at VCC - 2.1 V adds supply current ΔICC: up to 126 µA for a D or DS input and 234 µA for CP, /CE or /PL at 25 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Exposed pad",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "The centre pad of the DHVQFN16 package is not a ground connection and need not be soldered. If it is soldered, its land is left floating or tied to GND."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Standards and features",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Meets JESD8C (2.7 V to 3.6 V) and JESD7A (2.0 V to 6.0 V). Latch-up exceeds 100 mA under JESD 78 Class II Level B. Specified over -40 °C to +85 °C and -40 °C to +125 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Applications",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Converting parallel data to serial."
  }
 ],
